<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><meta name="description" content="Answers to the questions people might eventually ask about Glassmacs, Aquamacs, R, AI-assisted development and the roadmap."/><title>{title}</title><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg"/><style>:root{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif;color-scheme:light dark}body{margin:0;background:#f6f8fc;color:#182b42;line-height:1.7}header,main,footer{max-width:820px;margin:auto;padding:20px 26px}header{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:16px}a{color:#25628c}h1{font-size:clamp(2.5rem,6vw,4rem);line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.05em}.lead{font-size:1.15rem;color:#586b7f}.qa{border-top:1px solid #dce5ef;padding:18px 0 22px}.qa h2{font-size:1.27rem;margin:12px 0 6px}.qa p{margin:8px 0}.further{font-size:.94rem}footer{border-top:1px solid #dce5ef;margin-top:60px;font-size:.9rem}@media(prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{background:#101b2a;color:#ecf3fb}a{color:#8ec9f0}.lead{color:#b1c1d2}.qa,footer{border-color:#31455c}}</style></head><body><header><a href="/">← Glassmacs</a><nav><a href="/story/">Our story</a> · <a href="/roadmap/">Roadmap</a></nav></header><main><h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1><p class="lead">This page was written before anyone had actually started asking questions. These are the questions we think people may ask. Once real questions arrive, they will be allowed to compete with our guesses.</p><article class="qa"><h2>What is Glassmacs?</h2><p>An independent, open-source attempt to make contemporary GNU Emacs feel at home on macOS, particularly for R, ESS, and reproducible research.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">See what is implemented and what is planned. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Why build another Emacs for Mac?</h2><p>Because Aquamacs showed how valuable Mac conventions could be, but maintaining a heavily customized Emacs and keeping pace with macOS became difficult. Glassmacs tries a thinner layer around upstream GNU Emacs.</p><p class="further"><a href="/story/">Read the longer version. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Is Glassmacs Aquamacs 2.0?</h2><p>No. It is an homage, not an official successor, fork, or claim of complete feature parity. We are borrowing a philosophy and testing its modern implementation.</p><p class="further"><a href="/story/">Our Aquamacs appreciation. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Why is it called Glassmacs?</h2><p>Aquamacs was named for the Mac interface of its era. Apple's Liquid Glass design suggested Glassmacs. Liquidmacs would have been a regrettable alternative.</p><p class="further"><a href="/story/">The naming story. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Is it ready for everyday use?</h2><p>It is experimental. Automated tests cover substantial functionality, but clean-machine, release, and hands-on verification remain. Keep backups and do not assume beta-quality data safety yet.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">Current verification status. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Does it replace GNU Emacs?</h2><p>No. GNU Emacs is the editor. Glassmacs packages it with Mac-oriented behavior, configuration, and small native integrations. The intention is to stay close to upstream.</p><p class="further"><a href="/story/">Why a wrapper. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Will my normal Emacs configuration be overwritten?</h2><p>The design isolates Glassmacs state from ordinary Emacs configuration. That behavior is tested, but compatibility with every existing setup is not promised.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">Read the verification roadmap. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Can I use it without R?</h2><p>Yes. It is a text editor first, with R and research workflows receiving special attention. Not every optional research integration is complete.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">Feature coverage. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Does it include ESS?</h2><p>ESS integration is central, but integration and bundling are different things. Packaging and clean-machine availability still need explicit verification; don't assume every development build contains ESS.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">ESS packaging status. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Does it work with Quarto, R Markdown, and LaTeX?</h2><p>There are integrations for these workflows. Their end-to-end coverage varies, and the roadmap distinguishes code that exists from behavior that has been fully verified.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">Research features. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Will it reproduce every Aquamacs feature?</h2><p>Probably not—and that is intentional. We want useful behavioral parity, not the same maintenance burden. Differences should be documented rather than disguised.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">Aquamacs parity. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Why test against old Aquamacs bugs?</h2><p>Because years of bug reports are a valuable map of what can go wrong. AI-assisted development makes it practical to turn many of those reports into repeatable tests, while documenting what remains untested.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">Acceptance and regression work. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Was this app vibe coded?</h2><p>Extensively. Human-directed, AI-assisted development made both the new wrapper and the systematic regression effort feasible. The source and CI history are public; passing tests still matter more than the label.</p><p class="further"><a href="/story/">How it was built. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Was this website also written by AI?</h2><p>Yes, through iterative human–AI construction. The words are part of the project, and they deserve the same provenance disclosure as the code.</p><p class="further"><a href="/provenance/">How we label documentation. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Does that mean AI wrote every line?</h2><p>No. AI assistance can mean drafting, editing, testing, research, or repeated back-and-forth. A single authorship percentage would conceal more than it explains.</p><p class="further"><a href="/provenance/">Provenance labels. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>How can I tell whether a feature actually works?</h2><p>Look for acceptance evidence, not just a checkbox or an enthusiastic adjective. The project distinguishes unit tests, packaged GUI tests, and clean-machine/manual validation.</p><p class="further"><a href="/roadmap/">Feature evidence. →</a></p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Will Glassmacs finally fix Emacs keybindings?</h2><p>That depends on whether you believe they were broken. The goal is a configurable Aquamacs/macOS hybrid that preserves familiar Emacs commands.</p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Can it make my R code statistically significant?</h2><p>It can help you edit and run code. The p-value remains your responsibility.</p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Will it make my computer faster?</h2><p>The goal is a leaner, more maintainable Mac integration. Performance improvements must be measured, not inferred from a shiny name.</p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Is Liquid Glass involved in the code?</h2><p>Not as a magical programming substance. The name references Apple's interface design; GNU Emacs still does the editing.</p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Does it come with a free graduate student?</h2><p>No. We have not found a responsible way to package one.</p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Does the AI get to merge its own mistakes?</h2><p>Not without tests, review, and occasionally an embarrassing amount of debugging. Some of our most educational failures have been in the tests themselves.</p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Why are there so many words on this website?</h2><p>Because explaining the design and its limitations is part of earning trust. Also, the person directing the project is a professor.</p></article>
<article class="qa"><h2>Did we miss your question?</h2><p>Almost certainly. Questions, corrections, and bug reports are welcome through GitHub issues or email at software@joshpasek.com.</p></article></main><footer>Documentation provenance: Iterative human–AI construction. <a href="/provenance/">What that means</a>.</footer></body></html>